- Omega pattern: build relation on 2N points with order as described; verify transitivity+reflexivity; J(q_j) exactly; U_full true; no tail in J(q_j) for all m,j (since suffix from m contains x_{j+1} when j+1 ≤ N and j+1 ≥ m; if j = N and m ≤ N then suffix contains x_N ∈ J(q_N)! So avoid q_N in I+ or make j < N. Use I+ = {q_1..q_{N}} but then q_N dominates x_1..x_N. Hmm. Let's use I+ = {q_j : j = 1..N} and check tails: tail from m ⊆ J(q_j) iff all i ∈ [m,N] with i ≤ j, i.e., N ≤ j. So for j=N it holds! So to have no q see a tail, we need I+ = {q_1..q_{N-1}}... but then x_N ∉ union → U fails. The trick: the *infinite* structure has no maximum, so every q_j sees only finitely many x_i; truncation to N points with I+ limited to q_j (j < N) fails U for x_i with i > max j. The honest finite certificate is: a *window* W = {x_1..x_M, q_1..q_K} with K < M (so q's can't see the last window points), check (i) transitivity/past-closedness of the truncated relation, (ii) each x_i (i ≤ K) ∈ J(q_i) so "pointwise coverage holds on the covered part", (iii) no q_j sees any tail: for every m and every j ≤ K, x_{max(m,j)+1} exists in the window iff max(m,j)+1 ≤ M, and x_{max(m,j)+1} ⋠ q_j. Then the *extension lemma*: the pattern extends to the full infinite structure where U_full holds (every x_i ∈ J(q_i)). I'll verify the pattern for (M,K) = (8,4),(24,12),(64,32) and state the extension as a proof. Additionally verify "no q_j sees a tail" is not an artifact of truncation: for the infinite extension, for every j and m, x_{max(m,j)+1} ⋠ q_j because x_i ⪯ q_j iff i ≤ j. Good.

